ns corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $V$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${V}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-43004/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-43004"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75448/selection/0cdfd96c42ecae00258fbe24c7f10ec4e4b64b9e0b18ac8418f22cbb2ba5e190" :answer/id #uuid "fdd50191-b935-53c1-b729-05293a954016" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75448/selection/77ec4c31cc36952dda9ce10112776add9dbd83058a08a1c010a527337a9f1e84" :answer/id #uuid "f36e00ee-0bd4-52b0-ac41-d5b0c68c8ebb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75448/selection/49e062ce5a070074223ccb187baf384b502b21e973694f758e0c22372a06ce7f" :answer/id #uuid "ee539306-dc16-5c6a-ac64-c80f90300a7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75448/selection/8f1cceacfdbf4b000cf64444fd5957965cecacc4d556f8220e8af833d08ff5c1" :answer/id #uuid "0bc52ad0-43a8-5d07-a2e3-026c0d7bb13e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75448/selection/bcfa56d511b0b8dacc0a799bb27cd1f2ab1cddb408157c3bf8670a23b3d4e2de" :answer/id #uuid "c11f12e8-e827-59dd-bc86-d2966f0728ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75448/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "feeb800c-51ce-52bf-9086-c68c25f4bdc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75448/selection/49e062ce5a070074223ccb187baf384b502b21e973694f758e0c22372a06ce7f" :answer-field/choices ["q-75448/selection/0cdfd96c42ecae00258fbe24c7f10ec4e4b64b9e0b18ac8418f22cbb2ba5e190" "q-75448/selection/77ec4c31cc36952dda9ce10112776add9dbd83058a08a1c010a527337a9f1e84" "q-75448/selection/49e062ce5a070074223ccb187baf384b502b21e973694f758e0c22372a06ce7f" "q-75448/selection/8f1cceacfdbf4b000cf64444fd5957965cecacc4d556f8220e8af833d08ff5c1" "q-75448/selection/bcfa56d511b0b8dacc0a799bb27cd1f2ab1cddb408157c3bf8670a23b3d4e2de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75448" :question/id #uuid "08503628-ebe1-533e-ab77-0800c6924f29" :question/math-academy-id "q-75448" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-75448/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75448"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4725"] :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/e607bba88cc1f15abaa45fc3cf02110243433d60259c56805515ced373054ca0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4724"] :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/247c0a29294e61735b969d769d8ba3794c0b5e816c3968a8db087740eeb925d8.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4723"] :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/694b10f1d1c18f25e079e9e7cc75e9cc457ef01d0c229f006308fd667108459b.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
